findings.mutation:
Add to the head of ReaderInvariants.checkReader, after `List<ChainEntry> entries = Invariants.readChain(...)`:

    if (entries.isEmpty()) {
        return found;
    }

This is exactly the pre-M4.13i behaviour for one of the two arms. MEASURED: full :sequencer suite, 364 tests, BUILD SUCCESSFUL -- zero failures.

findings.failure_scenario:
A future change re-adds an emptiness short-circuit to checkReader (a plausible micro-optimisation: an epoch with no objects has nothing to iterate). checkReader then silently returns no violations for every never-opened epoch that has NO objects at all -- one of the two shapes M4.13's fault injection produces on purpose. M4.13's sweep counts each such seed as passing, so a reader that restarted every stream at 0 across a failover (I4) or applied a wild 500 (I3) is reported CLEAN while the invariant list records I3/I4 as CHECKED. That is the exact defect this row was written to close, and no test in the tree fails. Fix measured: adding to this test

    assertThat(ReaderInvariants.checkReader(store, PREFIX, new ReaderView(9, Map.of())))
            .extracting(Violation::invariant).containsExactly("I4");
    assertThat(ReaderInvariants.checkReader(store, PREFIX, new ReaderView(9, Map.of(RA, 500L))))
            .extracting(Violation::invariant).containsExactly("I3");

passes against the staged code and FAILS against the mutation above (365 tests, 1 failed).
